      "text_raw": "Full feedforward means that\nthe input signal is fed\ndirectly to the quantizer. As\na result, the noise transfer\nfunction H (z) is the same as\ne\nin a conventional topology\nbut the signal transfer func-\ntion H (z) is unity. This sug-\nx\ngests that the distortion is a\nlot less in the full-feedfor-\nward topology.\nIndeed, the signal goes\ndirectly to the quantizer\nwithout passing through the\nloop filter integrators. These\nfilters only process the\nquantization error, which is much smaller in amplitude than the signal itself. The distortion will\nbe a lot less indeed. The overload level can be higher and so is the dynamic range.",
      "text": "Full feedforward means that the input signal is fed directly to the quantizer. As a result, the noise transfer function H (z) is the same as e in a conventional topology but the signal transfer function H (z) is unity. This sugx gests that the distortion is a lot less in the full-feedforward topology. Indeed, the signal goes directly to the quantizer without passing through the loop filter integrators. These filters only process the quantization error, which is much smaller in amplitude than the signal itself. The distortion will be a lot less indeed. The overload level can be higher and so is the dynamic range."
    }
